1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,335
Can you show me StatefulSets?

2
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:12,104
A StatefulSet runs Pods that each have a stable identity:

3
00:00:12,104 --> 00:00:15,128
a fixed name (notes-0, notes-1, notes-2),

4
00:00:15,128 --> 00:00:20,881
a fixed DNS name, and their own disk that follows them when they are replaced.

5
00:00:20,881 --> 00:00:24,938
Pods are created in order and removed in reverse order.

6
00:00:24,938 --> 00:00:25,823
The analogy:

7
00:00:25,823 --> 00:00:28,626
Deployment Pods are like hotel guests:

8
00:00:28,626 --> 00:00:32,756
any room will do, and nobody minds who is in which room.

9
00:00:32,756 --> 00:00:37,255
StatefulSet Pods are like employees with a desk and a locker:

10
00:00:37,255 --> 00:00:40,869
notes-1 always comes back to locker data-notes-1.

11
00:00:41,433 --> 00:00:44,602
Deployments treat Pods as interchangeable:

12
00:00:44,602 --> 00:00:48,072
random names, shared or no storage, any order.

13
00:00:48,072 --> 00:00:51,316
That is perfect for a stateless web server.

14
00:00:51,316 --> 00:00:58,558
It breaks for databases and clustered systems (PostgreSQL replicas, Kafka, Elasticsearch, etcd):

15
00:00:58,558 --> 00:01:04,065
each member keeps its own data, the others must find it by a stable name,

16
00:01:04,065 --> 00:01:08,818
and the first member often has to start before the others join.

17
00:01:10,567 --> 00:01:12,986
And how does it actually work?

18
00:01:13,533 --> 00:01:13,996
Names:

19
00:01:13,996 --> 00:01:16,695
<statefulset>-<ordinal>, from 0 up.

20
00:01:16,695 --> 00:01:19,317
A replaced Pod gets the same name.

21
00:01:19,317 --> 00:01:19,779
Order:

22
00:01:19,779 --> 00:01:22,632
with the default podManagementPolicy:

23
00:01:22,632 --> 00:01:30,883
OrderedReady, notes-1 is created only when notes-0 is ready; scaling down removes the highest number first.

24
00:01:30,883 --> 00:01:31,500
Storage:

25
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:37,437
volumeClaimTemplates creates one PersistentVolumeClaim per Pod (data-notes-0,

26
00:01:37,437 --> 00:01:41,293
…), and the Pod with that number always mounts it.

27
00:01:43,033 --> 00:01:43,168
1.

28
00:01:43,168 --> 00:01:49,038
Create the namespace and the StatefulSet, and watch the Pods start one after the other:

29
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:49,795
2.

30
00:01:49,795 --> 00:01:52,910
Stable names, in creation order:

31
00:01:52,910 --> 00:01:53,105
3.

32
00:01:53,105 --> 00:01:54,760
One disk per Pod:

33
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:55,461
3.

34
00:01:55,461 --> 00:01:56,832
One disk per Pod:

35
00:01:56,832 --> 00:01:56,993
4.

36
00:01:56,993 --> 00:02:01,104
One DNS name per Pod, through the headless Service:

37
00:02:01,104 --> 00:02:04,489
ask for notes-1 by name from a client Pod.

38
00:02:05,033 --> 00:02:05,191
4.

39
00:02:05,191 --> 00:02:09,206
One DNS name per Pod, through the headless Service:

40
00:02:09,206 --> 00:02:12,513
ask for notes-1 by name from a client Pod.

41
00:02:14,267 --> 00:02:17,176
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

42
00:02:17,733 --> 00:02:23,983
The headless Service has no cluster IP, and DNS returns the three Pod addresses:

43
00:02:25,733 --> 00:02:27,645
Let me try something.

44
00:02:27,645 --> 00:02:34,108
A second StatefulSet, archive, asks for a StorageClass called fast-ssd:

45
00:02:34,667 --> 00:02:36,387
What should happen?

46
00:02:36,387 --> 00:02:37,746
archive-0 runs.

47
00:02:37,746 --> 00:02:39,014
What happened?

48
00:02:39,014 --> 00:02:42,726
It is Pending, and only archive-0 exists:

49
00:02:42,726 --> 00:02:47,164
a StatefulSet waits for each Pod before the next.

50
00:02:47,164 --> 00:02:49,971
Which object is it waiting for?

51
00:02:51,733 --> 00:02:56,712
volumeClaimTemplates cannot be changed on an existing StatefulSet,

52
00:02:56,712 --> 00:03:00,710
and the PVC it already created keeps the wrong class.

53
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:08,028
Delete both (the StatefulSet first, so no Pod uses the PVC), then create it with the right class:

54
00:03:09,767 --> 00:03:10,480
Got it.

55
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:13,231
What do I need to remember?

56
00:03:13,767 --> 00:03:22,611
StatefulSet = stable names (name-0…), ordered start/stop, one PVC per Pod that survives the Pod.

57
00:03:23,167 --> 00:03:26,222
A headless Service (clusterIP:

58
00:03:26,222 --> 00:03:29,482
None) gives each Pod a DNS name:

59
00:03:29,482 --> 00:03:33,556
pod.service.namespace.svc.cluster.local.

60
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:37,123
PVCs outlive Pods and the StatefulSet:

61
00:03:37,123 --> 00:03:40,385
deleting data is always an explicit step.
